On 20 May 2004 13:08:10 -0700, wcristalle@hotmail.com (Artemis the
Ranger) wrote:

>The recent discussion on how magic works in Nethack got me thinking.
>I've been playing a wizard recently (if all goes well, an ascension's
>on the way) and I'd like to see a few new spells, just to keep life
>interesting. Some ideas:
>
>Create Illusion (level 3, enchantment): creates an illusion of either
>an object or  a dungeon feature (e.g. boulder, wall.) If an object,
>you (and other monsters) can pick it up and hold it, but it has none
>of its normal functions. Thus, zapping an illusionary wand will give
>"Nothing happens"; a weapon will do no damage when wielded or thrown;
>rings and amulets will have no effects (and will not cause increased
>digestion); and so on. Monsters (including shopkeepers) will be fooled
>by the illusions (perhaps given an Int-dependent saving throw);
>hostile monsters will attempt to pick up the objects and use them (if
>they would normally pick up objects of that type.) If an illusion of a
>dungeon feature (e.g. a wall blocking a tunnel) is created, monsters
>will be fooled by the illusions (e.g. will not attempt to walk through
>the wall). As the creator of the illusion, *you* recognize it for what
>it is and are never fooled. After a set number of turns, the illusion
>vanishes.

Not sure, but this sounds absolutely wicked hard to implement, as
you'd have to keep track of which monsters believed the illusions,
which ones didn't, and create a new flag for every object and wall as
to whether it was real or not.

>Create Double (level 7, matter or clerical): A real (not illusionary)
>double of you is created beside you. It is wielding a weapon identical
>to yours, is dressed in the same armor, and is wearing the same
>rings/amulets, but has no other possessions. It has identical
>intrinsics and stats to those you possessed at the time of creation.
>It is tame and will fight any nearby monsters in melee combat, but
>cannot cast spells (as this would be difficult to code.) After a short
>amount of time (30-50 turns), or when it is killed, it vanishes,
>leaving nothing behind.

Again, a can of worms, I'd think; how would you handle
leprechaun/nymph/footrice attacks?

>Snatch (level 3, matter): allows you to snatch away one random object
>held by a monster. The monster must be visible, in a direct line of
>sight, and no more than five squares away. The object must be in the
>monster's main inventory, and must not be too heavy (i.e. no boulders,
>chests, or bronze plate mail.) If the object is wielded/worn, the
>spell has a 50% chance of failure; otherwise, a 5% chance of failure.
>The object flies towards you; you have a Dex-dependent chance of
>catching it. Otherwise, the object will either hit you, inflicting
>appropriate damage and other relevant effects, or will sail past you
>and crash on the dungeon floor behind you (this will, of course, cause
>fragile objects to shatter.) Furthermore, the monster (if it has
>hands) has a Dex-dependent chance of grabbing the object out of the
>air as it flies towards you.

I like this one, but I think it's too powerful. Maybe only for
adjacent monsters?

